{"bullets":["Urban heat refers to the phenomenon where urban areas experience higher temperatures than their rural surroundings due to the concentration of buildings, roads, and other infrastructure.","The heat island effect is a major contributor to urban heat, where surfaces like asphalt and concrete absorb and retain heat, leading to increased ambient temperatures.","Studies show that urban areas can be several degrees warmer than nearby rural areas, especially during heatwaves.","Mitigation strategies include increasing green spaces, using reflective materials in construction, and improving urban planning to promote airflow and reduce heat retention.","Urban heat poses significant risks to public health, energy consumption, and environmental sustainability, making it a critical area of research and policy development."],"status":"written","title":"Urban-Heat"}